{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And Moses said to them: If you do… if you arm yourselves…</b> It is difficult: why did he repeat, saying <b>if you do</b> etc. <b>if you arm yourselves</b> etc., and not suffice with one of them? And when we examine closely, he should not have needed to reply to them at all, but rather should have agreed to their words by calling to Joshua and Elazar and commanding them, as stated in the adjoining passage. Further, why did he precisely say ״this.״ Further, what is his intention in saying, in the adjoining passage, ״before HaShem״?" "<b>Now</b> the intention of Moses' statement is this: for he saw that the disposition of the children of Gad and the children of Reuben — that they want to cross over with Israel — is only so that [the leaders] will be appeased and will finalize for them the granting of the lands, and not for any other purpose. And this matter is not proper to do with respect to the success of the war, for the men of war need their warfare to be for the purpose of taking vengeance against the enemies of HaShem, to destroy from them every soul, and it is called a war of the obligation of HaShem's commandment. And this is the language of Rambam in chapter 7 of the Laws of Kings: ״Anyone who fights with all his heart…״ etc., and his intention should be to sanctify the Name alone; he is assured that no harm will befall him and no evil will reach him״ etc., ״and he will merit life in the World to Come,״ end quote." "<b>Therefore</b> those men — since they are going only to secure the lands through their own hands — there is reason for concern that their arming themselves will not avail, and it is possible that they will be imperiled by them [the enemy]. Therefore Moses acted wisely and said to them <b>if you do</b> etc. <b>if you arm yourselves</b> etc. — meaning, corresponding to what they themselves said, ״we will arm ourselves swiftly״ etc., he said ״if you do this thing,״ and the word ״this״ refers back to the statement said in the adjoining passage. And corresponding to what their intention was in the march — [namely,] for the fulfillment of the condition — he said ״if you arm yourselves before HaShem for the war.״ He was precise in saying <b>before HaShem</b>, in accordance with Rambam's statement that he wrote, that one's intention should be to sanctify HaShem, and as he concluded saying <b>until He dispossesses His enemies</b> — meaning, the enemies of the Holy One, blessed be He. And one should not interpret ״His enemies״ as [the enemies] of Israel, for indeed from the beginning of Moses' words until now the mention of Israel has not appeared, to say that the statement of ״His enemies״ refers back to Israel. Rather, it is certain that the matter refers back to HaShem — that their intention should be to take vengeance against the enemies of HaShem, to sanctify His Name, blessed be He. And according to this interpretation we have resolved the framing of Moses' words whose place [of reference] was not known, that all the statements he placed before them begin with a vav at their beginning, and according to our words the framing is ״before HaShem.״" "<b>Further,</b> he intended in saying <b>if you arm yourselves before HaShem</b> to reply to them regarding what is understood from their words, when they said ״we will arm ourselves… before the children of Israel,״ which indicates that Israel need natural assistance. Therefore he said ״if you arm yourselves before HaShem״ — ״תֵּחָלְצוּ,״ meaning, to multiply your merit before HaShem, for the merit of one hundred is not equivalent to the merit of one hundred and one."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:16:52.307374915Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}